  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA surface low will continue to drop southeast into the Gulf of\nMaine this morning with the upper low close on it's heels. This\nwill lead to another round of showers rotating north to south\nthrough the forecast area from around 07z to 15z or so this\nmorning. The lowest probabilities for showers this morning will\nbe across the Kennebec River Valley and Midcoast areas which\nwill be further east than the best synoptic forcing. While\nthunder will not be as widespread, a few rumbles will be\npossible with a little bit of instability floating around.\n\nMost of the precipitation will move out in the morning, but we\ncould have a few more diurnal showers develop in the afternoon.\nThere will be some modest instability again, so a thunderstorm\nor two is not out of the question but coverage will also be \nlimited. \n\nThis morning, lows will mainly range from the upper 30s north, \nto the low/mid 40s south. Afternoon highs will be on the cooler \nside once again, ranging from the upper 50s to the mid 60s. Lows\ntonight will then be similar to this morning, in the mid to \nupper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe upper-level troughing finally releases its grip on New England \nby Tuesday. This happens as the omega block pattern breaks down, and \nthe anomalous ridge over Canada is squashed southeast into the \nNortheast U.S. For local sensible weather, this should result in a \nmulti-day period of tranquil conditions and near seasonable \ntemperatures as a 1025 mb high pressure crests over the region. Flow \nwill be light so sea breeze formation will be possible each day \nTuesday onward. Much of the week will feature highs in the 70s, but \nthere is a chance temperatures could reach the 80s by Thursday-\nFriday as it becomes warmer aloft. It should generally be a pleasant \nairmass with dew points in the 50s, but humidity may become a bit \nmore noticeable by next weekend depending on the shortwave's \ntiming.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThere are chances for precipitation on Tuesday and next \nweekend, but neither day looks to be widespread or long duration\nchances. On Tuesday, the remnants of the weekend's cold pool \nwill still be in the vicinity. Hi-res guidance coming into range\nis showing rather steep low-level lapse rates ~9.0 C/Km, paired\nwith freezing levels still hovering around 800 mb. This looks \nto be enough to generate a few hundred J/Kg of CAPE, which may \nbe enough for downpours and isolated thunderstorms.",
